"A catalogue, keyed within a tenant: a catalogue code means nothing on its own."
type Catalogue implements Node @node @table(name: "catalogue") { id: ID! @nodeId }
"A shelf, keyed the same way. An item may sit on one or on none."
type CatalogueShelf implements Node @node @table(name: "catalogue_shelf") { id: ID! @nodeId }
"""
An item, keyed (tenant_id, item_no). Both foreign keys leaving it carry the tenant column, because
a catalogue code and a shelf code are each unique only within a tenant, so every reference from
this table straddles its own key.
"""
type CatalogueItem implements Node @node @table(name: "catalogue_item") {
  id: ID! @nodeId
  itemName: String @field(name: "item_name")
}

"""
Two straddling references and no whole carrier for the tenant column, which is the shape that
separates the two ways this rule can be read. `catalogueId` is non-null: its in-key half wins the
claim and becomes the statement's tenant predicate, and a non-null field cannot be absent, so it is
as good a supplier as a field of the row's own would be. `shelfId` is optional and is admitted
because of that: it neither filters nor writes the tenant column, and an explicit null on it clears
`shelf_code` alone.
"""
input RepointCatalogueItemInput {
  itemNo: Int! @field(name: "item_no")
  itemName: String @field(name: "item_name")
  catalogueId: ID! @nodeId(typeName: "Catalogue")
  shelfId: ID @nodeId(typeName: "CatalogueShelf")
}

"""
The same input with the non-null reference taken away, which is the one shape that cannot work.
Nothing else supplies the tenant column now, so the optional reference would have to be the
statement's tenant predicate, and an omitted value leaves no way to find the row at all.
"""
input ShelveCatalogueItemInput {
  itemNo: Int! @field(name: "item_no")
  itemName: String @field(name: "item_name")
  shelfId: ID @nodeId(typeName: "CatalogueShelf")
}

type Mutation {
  repointCatalogueItem(in: RepointCatalogueItemInput!): CatalogueItem
    @mutation(typeName: UPDATE)
    @classified(source: Mutation, operations: [Update, Reentry], target: Single, targetShape: Table)

  shelveCatalogueItem(in: ShelveCatalogueItemInput!): CatalogueItem
    @mutation(typeName: UPDATE)
}

extend schema @expectEquals(relation: "intent_mutation_write_refusal", rows: """
  type_name, field_name,          input_field_name, cause
  Mutation,  shelveCatalogueItem, shelfId,          NULLABLE_STRADDLING_REFERENCE
  """)
